<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Find Intercepts: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02073</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Find Intercepts: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>587b7ed7-e2c3-40dd-8412-dc5c53fa7741</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Find the \(x\)-Intercepts</title>
<para>The \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of an equation occur when \(y = 0\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>For the equation \(y=(x-9)(x+2)\), look at the factors \((x-9)\) and \((x+2)\).
  Notice in the first factor, \((x-9)\), \(x=9\) makes the factor equal to 0.
  Substitute 9 for \(x\) in the equation.</para>
<para>\(y=(9-9)(9+2)\)
  \(y=(0)(11)\)
  \(y=0\)</para>
<para>Notice in the second factor, \((x+2)\), \(x=-2\) makes the factor equal to 0.
  Substitute -2 for \(x\) in the equation.</para>
<para>\(y=(-2-9)(-2+2)\)
  \(y=(-11)(0)\)
  \(y=0\)</para>
<para>In the equation \(y=(x-9)(x+2)\), \(y=0\) when \(x=9\) and \(x=-2\).
  The \(x\)-intercepts occur when \(x=9\) and \(x=-2\).
  To find the \(x\)-intercepts of a quadratic equation in factored form, set each factor equal to zero and solve for \(x\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the \(x\)-intercepts for \(y=(2x+7)(x-4)\).</para>
<para>The \(x\)-intercepts occur when \(y = 0\).
  Set each factor to 0 and solve.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+7&amp;=&amp;0\;\;\;\;\;x-4&amp;=&amp;0\\x&amp;=&amp;-3.5\;\;\;\;\;\;x&amp;=&amp;4\end{array}\)</para>
<title>Try It: Find the \(x\)-Intercepts</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Find the \(x\)-intercepts for \(y=(2x-5)(x+3)\).</para>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to find the \(x\)-intercepts:</para>
  
  <para>The \(x\)-intercepts occur when \(y = 0\).
    Set each factor to 0 and solve.</para>
  
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x-5&amp;=&amp;0\;\;\;\;\;x+3&amp;=&amp;0\\x&amp;=&amp;2.5\;\;\;\;\;\;x&amp;=&amp;-3\end{array}\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><title>Check Your Understanding</title><exercise data-content-id="c70e3466-ddbb-46c7-8eb3-670add88a2bd" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What are the \(x\)-intercept(s) of the graph of \(y=(2x+1)(x-3)\)?</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = -3\), \(x = 0.5\)</para><para>\(x = 3\), \(x = -0.5\)</para><para>\(x = -3\), \(x = 1\)</para><para>\(x = 3\), \(x = -1\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Take a moment to think about what you learned in the mini-lesson review. </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
